  "body": "**What.** A mapped Fourier basis in \u03c4 that moves the profile's pole pair away from the real axis, a strip or ellipse map tied to the pole positions of Eq. (16) or its rational generalization. Test whether it cuts the \u03c4 resolution M the solver needs, first at large D, then below D \u2248 3.1, where M = 320\u2013384 now.\n\n**Why.** The pole distance \u00bd ln(1/q) is what limits Fourier convergence. A model of where the poles sit gives the map. Low D is where it matters: the profile steepens and the modes needed keep growing (#93).\n\n**Acceptance (can fail).** A planted profile with known poles needs fewer modes, under the map, for the same error, with the criterion written first. On stored states at D = 60 and at D 3.05\u20133.2, the M needed for a given \u0394 error with and without the map is tabulated. A map tied to the wrong poles (a control) must do worse than none.\n\n**Depends on.** #133 (the rational family's poles), #136 (\u03c3(x)), or the low-D singularity measurement of #93.\n",
